### What are the differences between Predicate, Function, Supplier, and Consumer?

### How does each interface work?

| Interface | Signature | Input | Output | Purpose | Stream Usage |
|-----------|-----------|-------|--------|---------|-------------|
| Predicate<T> | boolean test(T) | T | boolean | Filter/condition | filter() |
| Function<T,R> | R apply(T) | T | R | Transform/map | map() |
| Consumer<T> | void accept(T) | T | void | Side effect | forEach() |
| Supplier<T> | T get() | none | T | Factory/lazy | generate() |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.function.*;

public class FourPillarsDemo {
    record Account(String id, String holder, BigDecimal balance, boolean active) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Account> accounts = List.of(
            new Account("SAV2001", "Rajesh", new BigDecimal("125000"), true),
            new Account("SAV2002", "Priya", new BigDecimal("8000"), false),
            new Account("CUR3001", "Amit", new BigDecimal("250000"), true)
        );

        // Predicate: T -> boolean
        Predicate<Account> isActive = Account::active;
        Predicate<Account> isLarge = a -> a.balance().compareTo(new BigDecimal("100000")) > 0;

        // Function: T -> R
        Function<Account, String> toSummary = a -> a.id() + ": Rs." + a.balance();

        // Consumer: T -> void
        Consumer<Account> logAccount = a -> System.out.println("Processed: " + a.id());

        // Supplier: () -> T
        Supplier<String> txnId = () -> "TXN-" + System.currentTimeMillis();

        // All four in one pipeline:
        System.out.println("Report ID: " + txnId.get());
        accounts.stream()
            .filter(isActive.and(isLarge))    // Predicate
            .map(toSummary)                    // Function
            .forEach(System.out::println);     // Consumer
    }
}
```

```text
Report ID: TXN-1705312800000
SAV2001: Rs.125000
CUR3001: Rs.250000
```

### How does composition work for each?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.function.*;

public class CompositionDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Predicate composition: and, or, negate
        Predicate<BigDecimal> positive = amt -> amt.signum() > 0;
        Predicate<BigDecimal> large = amt -> amt.compareTo(new BigDecimal("100000")) > 0;
        Predicate<BigDecimal> positiveLarge = positive.and(large);   // AND
        Predicate<BigDecimal> smallOrNeg = positiveLarge.negate();    // NOT
        Predicate<BigDecimal> positiveOrLarge = positive.or(large);   // OR

        // Function composition: andThen, compose
        Function<String, BigDecimal> parse = BigDecimal::new;
        Function<BigDecimal, String> format = amt -> "Rs." + amt.toPlainString();
        Function<String, String> parseAndFormat = parse.andThen(format); // parse first, then format
        System.out.println(parseAndFormat.apply("50000")); // Rs.50000

        // Consumer composition: andThen
        Consumer<String> log = msg -> System.out.println("LOG: " + msg);
        Consumer<String> audit = msg -> System.out.println("AUDIT: " + msg);
        Consumer<String> both = log.andThen(audit); // log first, then audit
        both.accept("Transfer completed");
    }
}
```

```text
Rs.50000
LOG: Transfer completed
AUDIT: Transfer completed
```

### What is the extended family?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.function.*;

public class ExtendedFamilyDemo {
    record Account(String id, BigDecimal balance) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // BiFunction: two inputs -> one output
        BiFunction<Account, BigDecimal, Account> applyInterest =
            (acc, rate) -> new Account(acc.id(), acc.balance().multiply(rate.add(BigDecimal.ONE)));

        // UnaryOperator: same type in and out (extends Function<T,T>)
        UnaryOperator<BigDecimal> applyGST = amt -> amt.multiply(new BigDecimal("1.18"));

        // BinaryOperator: same type reduction (extends BiFunction<T,T,T>)
        BinaryOperator<BigDecimal> sum = BigDecimal::add;
        BigDecimal total = java.util.List.of(
            new BigDecimal("1000"), new BigDecimal("2000"), new BigDecimal("3000")
        ).stream().reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, sum);

        System.out.println("Total: Rs." + total);
        System.out.println("With GST: Rs." + applyGST.apply(total));
    }
}
```

```text
Total: Rs.6000
With GST: Rs.7080.00
```

### Where is each used in the Stream API?

| Interface | Stream Method | Example |
|-----------|--------------|--------|
| Predicate | filter() | `.filter(Account::active)` |
| Function | map() | `.map(Account::balance)` |
| Consumer | forEach() | `.forEach(System.out::println)` |
| Supplier | generate() | `Stream.generate(UUID::randomUUID)` |
| BinaryOperator | reduce() | `.reduce(ZERO, BigDecimal::add)` |
| Comparator | sorted() | `.sorted(Comparator.comparing(...))` |

### What are common mistakes?

Using Consumer where Function is needed (returning void when a value is expected). Confusing Function and UnaryOperator (use UnaryOperator when input and output types are the same). Using Supplier with expensive operations in orElse instead of orElseGet.

### What should a senior engineer know?

The seven core interfaces plus their Bi variants cover all common lambda shapes. Creating custom functional interfaces is appropriate only when the domain name adds clarity. Named predicates stored as static factory methods are reusable and testable. Composition replaces class hierarchies for combining simple behaviours into complex ones.
